{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy","id":1513630,"title":"Questions / reflections about law and moral philosophy","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-06-02T04:30:29-07:00","id":1513630,"title":"Questions / reflections about law and moral philosophy","content":"<span style=\"color:#2c3e50\">A couple things I'm actually debating on Quora too.<br /> <br /> Hypotethical question 1.<br /> <br /> * A man has been convicted for the crime of <b>murder</b>.<br /> * The sentence is <b>death by lethal injection</b>.<br /> * He's<b> innocent</b>, so for the spirit of the law his execution is not legit. He did not kill anyone.<br /> * He manages to escape the prison but in doing so he kills several guards.<br /> * He had no choice - if he wanted to escape - but to kill those guards. If he instead surrendered, he would have been captured and then executed.<br /> <br /> Is the man morally justified or excusable for his actions?<br /> <br /> - - -<br /> <br /> Hypotethical question 2<br /> <br /> * A man has been convicted for the crime of <b>murder</b>.<br /> * The sentence is <b>death by lethal injection</b>.<br /> * He's <b>guilty</b>, so for the spirit of the law his execution is legit. He killed a person.<br /> * He manages to escape the prison but in doing so he kills several guards.<br /> * He had no choice - if he wanted to escape - but to kill those guards. If he instead surrendered, he would have been captured and then executed.<br /> <br /> Is the man morally justified or excusable for his actions?<br /> <br /> - - -<br /> <br /> Hypotethical question 3<br /> <br /> * A man has been convicted for the crime of <b>murder</b>.<br /> * The sentence is <b>life in prison without parole</b>.<br /> * He's <b>innocent</b>, so for the spirit of the law the conviction is not legit. He did not kill anyone.<br /> * He manages to escape the prison but in doing so he kills several guards.<br /> * He had no choice - if he wanted to escape - but to kill those guards. If he instead surrendered, he would have been captured and then imprisoned for life.<br /> <br /> Is the man morally justified or excusable for his actions?</span>\u200b<br /> \u200b<br /> - - -<br /> <br /> Hypotetical question 4<br /> <br /> * A woman has been convicted for the crime of <b>refusing an imposed wedding</b>.<br /> * The sentence is <b>being repeatedly raped, then killed by stoning</b>.<br /> * She's<b> guilty,</b> so for the spirit of the law ( of her country ) her execution is legit. She truly refused the wedding.<br /> <span style=\"color:#2c3e50\">* She manages to escape the prison but in doing so he kills several guards.<br /> * She had no choice - if he wanted to escape - but to kill those guards. If she instead surrendered, he would have been captured and then executed.<br /> <br /> Is the woman morally justified or excusable for her actions?<br /> <br /> - - -</span><br /> <br /> Hypotetical question 5<br /> <br /> * A woman has been convicted for the crime of <b>murder.</b>.<br /> * The sentence is <b>being repeatedly raped, then killed by stoning</b>.<br /> * She's<b> guilty,</b> so for the spirit of the law ( of her country ) her execution is legit. She killed a person.<br /> <span style=\"color:#2c3e50\">* She manages to escape the prison but in doing so he kills several guards.<br /> * She had no choice - if he wanted to escape - but to kill those guards. If she instead surrendered, he would have been captured and then executed.<br /> <br /> Is the woman morally justified or excusable for her actions?</span>\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8595,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8595-conradine","user":"Conradine","date":". 06-02-2023, 08:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8595-conradine","name":"Conradine","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8595_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-02T05:05:04-07:00","id":1513633,"title":"","content":"Yes to all. As an American, I think a lot of people confuse the law with justice, due to the deliberate effort of propagandists who have convinced so many of us to refer to the law as &quot;the justice system.&quot; It's not a justice system, and I don't believe was ever intended to be one. The legal system is about maintaining power via the government's claim on the monopoly of violence. You have the right to use proportionate violence against people who are using it against you; and again, as an American, I don't believe a for-profit institution that makes money by legalized slavery has any moral high ground over any of the people enslaved therein, not even the one guilty murderer.<br /> <br /> If we were talking about some other country's laws, in a place where the penal system is more just and isn't about becoming a plantation for the state and/or shareholders to profit from, then I might change my mind depending on the circumstances about the person imprisoned for life. The people sentenced to death still have every right not to allow themselves to die, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/271-jimb","name":"JimB","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar271_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-09T14:51:11-07:00","id":1514339,"title":"","content":"According to Plato, they would not be morally justified because they are being disobedient to the State and even though the State had erred, it would not be virtuous to defy the will of the people. There's also a hint of utilitarianism, in that the greater good is the the maximum amount of people that experience good. Also, killing the guards, even to save his own life, does in fact, make him a murderer.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-09T16:54:20-07:00","id":1514357,"title":"","content":"I think a lot of these questions suffer from compounding morally questions without some sort of baseline being set first.<br /> <br /> As Jim noted, there's a not great assumption that &quot;spirit of the law,&quot; has an inherent moral judgement. Especially with these examples, we need to examine the system these punishments are being doled out by. Most people agree there are extreme moral issues with the death penalty in general as a form of punishment, and very few modern philosophers will agree with the idea that state sanctioned torture is morally excusable just because someone broke the law (which definitely applies to 4 and 5, and lots of people would argue 3 as well). What to do about unjust punishments is also extremely contextual. Facing life in prison for a false conviction, but having 15 peaceful avenues of seeking justice is not the same as facing execution after a kangaroo court with no avenue of peaceful redress of your case.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-10T03:44:43-07:00","id":1514442,"title":"","content":"In all of the examples except number 2 the person is morally justified in doing what they did. And in number 2 the person probably doesn\u2019t lose much sleep wondering if their actions were morally justified.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-10T06:54:22-07:00","id":1514465,"title":"","content":"I think 1 and 3 would at least require some level of immediacy to consider quite so simply. The conditions stated don't address time, the ability to appeal, or the ability to petition for clemency.<br /> <br /> If you heard a man that was factually innocent but was convicted through some mechanism decided to murder his way out of prison before his lawyer had a chance to file an appeal, I doubt you'd say, &quot;yeah, that's justified.&quot;<br /> <br /> These things also don't really get into what &quot;escape&quot; looks like, as they only stop at escaping imprisonment. Are thy going to be wanted fugitives for the rest of their lives? Is 3 really justified in killing people to escape if he escapes to a life of hiding in a cave with worse conditions than life in prison but he's &quot;free?&quot; I wouldn't agree it's that simple.<br /> <br /> <br /> --------<br /> <br /> Basically, all of these questions suffer one of the key issues with the infamous &quot;Trolley Car&quot; setup: that you can't just kill the person that keeps tying people to trolley tracks and makes people pick who lives and dies, so you can rescue them all instead. The attempt to confine the problem to make a philosophic point ends up invalidating the point by being too divorced from reality.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-14T00:35:58-07:00","id":1514925,"title":"","content":"It's fascinating to consider the ethics and justification behind the actions of the individuals in each scenario.For the first scenario, where the man is innocent and facing an unjust execution, one could argue that his actions of killing the guards to escape were morally justified. He had no other option if he wanted to avoid an unjust death. However, taking human lives is a serious matter, and it's still a complex and controversial topic. In college I had project on this topic and get some guide from <a href=\"https://www.personalstatementwriter.org/\" target=\"_blank\">statement writer\u200b</a> In the second scenario, where the man is guilty and facing a legitimate execution, the situation becomes murkier. While his actions of killing the guards may have been necessary for his escape, it's difficult to argue that they were morally justified. After all, he committed a crime and was rightfully sentenced to death. Taking more lives in his attempt to escape does not erase his initial guilt. The third scenario adds another layer of complexity. If the man is innocent but sentenced to life in prison, his actions of killing the guards to escape could be seen as a desperate act to regain his freedom. However, whether his actions are morally justified or excusable is still a matter of debate. Ultimately, these scenarios raise thought-provoking questions about the nature of justice, morality, and the value of human life. Different perspectives and ethical frameworks may lead to diverse interpretations and conclusions.\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":27939,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27939-dazert1","user":"Dazert1","date":". 06-19-2023, 06:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27939-dazert1","name":"Dazert1","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27939_1.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"21"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-14T01:34:06-07:00","id":1514928,"title":"","content":"While of course different schools of thought are going to have different answers here, but I think the scenarios posed are a bit more provocative in imagination than in ethical depth.<br /> <br /> There's basically just two questions being asked:<br /> <br /> 1) Is it acceptable to act in an unjust fashion in response to the actions an unjust system?<br /> 2) When does a society's actions make it unjust?<br /> <br /> The stakes of the scenarios do more to elicit emotional responses over ethical ones. For example the morality and ethical concerns of simply breaking out of prison without actively harming anyone got completely skipped over.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-24T11:31:49-07:00","id":1516030,"title":"","content":"It's skipped over because the act of simply breaking out of prison without harming anyone , if done to avoid execution, is considered by most people legit or at least justifiable.<br /> Not even death penalty supporters think that a prisoner sentenced to die should refrain from escaping if given a clean chance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8595-conradine","name":"Conradine","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8595_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-24T14:19:00-07:00","id":1516041,"title":"","content":"Do you... have any credible source for that claim? There's a huge difference between, &quot;I would run even if it would be immoral or unethical to save my life,&quot; and, &quot;it's moral/ethical to escape just punishment.&quot; Morals and ethics aren't built on people being able to follow them perfectly.<br /> <br /> This is also going about any discussion of ethics entirely backwards. Whether as a debate, or to be informed as to other ways of thinking on ethics, you start without foundational principles and work up to complex scenarios.<br /> <br /> You're also not really doing much to assuage my feelings that the examples are framed to get emotional responses over considered moral or ethical ones.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-24T22:14:38-07:00","id":1516061,"title":"","content":"Apparently I\u2019m going to go against the grain, but I think murder is morally wrong in every situation. Doesn\u2019t matter if it\u2019s by a person or a state. It just happens to be very easy and permanent way to deal with people. Law isn\u2019t inherently moral, and arguably punishment like we frame it in the justice system is less than stellar at actually preventing crime or protecting people. So uh, like none of the listed examples are morally just. On either side.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>To whomever reads this, I hope you have a good day/night. May you be Happy.<br /> <br /> So, I made some Mage Legacies <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1497183-templebuilder-s-new-legacy-thread#post1497183\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>, with some help. They vary in quality, but I hope you take a look at them. Every one contains pieces of me, for better or worse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26736-templebuilder","name":"TempleBuilder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26736_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1251"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-24T23:21:20-07:00","id":1516064,"title":"","content":"If this thread is going to insist on continuation, then I want to ask what the end goal of the OP is with these questions, since that's going to have a significant -idle moral discussion as entertainment is markedly different then trying to wrap your head around complex issues with moving parts, as an example.<br /> <br /> And no, lots of people are not okay with people just escaping from jail, that's not a thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-25T04:51:20-07:00","id":1516090,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TempleBuilder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516061#post1516061\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Apparently I\u2019m going to go against the grain, but I think murder is morally wrong in every situation.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's somewhat tautological by calling it murder in the first place. &quot;Murder&quot; already means, &quot;killing we consider wrong to do.&quot; There are exceedingly few moral philosophies that go to the extremes of &quot;all homicide is murder regardless of context.&quot; A fairly common example would be self-defense If a person were attacked by three others, two to hold and one that's clearly just going to kill, the victim defending themselves with lethal force - including against the two just holding them - wouldn't be considered immoral in most philosophies. It's frequently recognized that absolute pacifism doesn't really work in a world with people that don't care about how they look to others if they use brutal violence to get their way (or even like it) <br /> <br /> It's not such a large leap from the scenario I proposed, to killing a guard to escape an unjust execution: at least from the wrongly convicted individual's perspective, the guards are knowingly abetting the attempt by the state to murder them; they're people holding an innocent person down before the executioner arrives.<br /> <br /> The examples were also purposefully stated to remove the context of the jurisdiction, and its history of successfully being a system of justice vs, one of being a system of state violence meant to control the population.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-25T10:42:24-07:00","id":1516111,"title":"","content":"Right. Allow me to rephrase then. Killing humans is morally unjust in my opinion. So every killing (unless it was accidental) would be murder. In <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> example, I would still consider it unjustified morally, since there are ways to incapacitate attackers without killing them. It\u2019s justified by practicality, most people don\u2019t have the ability to do that, and even for masters of incapacitation, is it really better odds for one\u2019s survival then just killing them? Never mind that no means of incapacitation is perfectly safe.<br /> <br /> I wouldn\u2019t be surprised if my morality differs from many philosophies. I made it, from my understanding of the world. It probably has a lot of holes, and may be somewhat incomprehensible to others, but it\u2019s mine.<br /> <br /> On the viability of absolute pacifism in the real world, well yeah, I\u2019d expect it doesn\u2019t do well on a practical level when faced with its counterpart. Morality is not grounded in the real, it\u2019s grounded in the ideal. It\u2019s up to us to choose how to translate that into the real world.<br /> <br /> I guess, let me phrase it like this. There is a bomb inside a building, which is designed to go off after 10 minutes. This bomb will collapse the building, killing everyone inside. There are a large number of people in the building, around 200. Enough people where trying to evacuate everyone before the bomb goes off will leave some behind. Which is the more moral action? To try and defuse the bomb, and save everyone? Or to evacuate and save who you can?<br /> <br /> Is it more moral to try for the best outcome? Or more moral to try to avert the worst outcome? <br /> <br /> Edit: refined the example.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>To whomever reads this, I hope you have a good day/night. May you be Happy.<br /> <br /> So, I made some Mage Legacies <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1497183-templebuilder-s-new-legacy-thread#post1497183\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>, with some help. They vary in quality, but I hope you take a look at them. Every one contains pieces of me, for better or worse.","edited":{"userid":26736,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26736-templebuilder","user":"TempleBuilder","date":". 06-25-2023, 02:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26736-templebuilder","name":"TempleBuilder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26736_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1251"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-25T13:05:04-07:00","id":1516126,"title":"","content":"The example is a little more on point when the bomb is, in fact, a person.<br /> <br /> Murder is definitely a drop in pile of bad things, and no amount of drops into the pile of good things changes that fact-but if you get hung up on that, people will take advantage, and it also denies that drops do get into the pile of good things.<br /> <br /> The biggest problem with murder is how easy it can be to end up justifying it, to forget that yes, it is always a failure that should be mourned to have been committed-but it's possible to make no mistakes and still lose (that's life). It's nothing that should ever be arrived at lightly-but with the world as it is, arriving at it is inevitable in a lot of circumstances.<br /> <br /> Like a bomb, it is ideal to defuse any given situation and work towards a better scenario, but there are times &quot;defusal&quot; absolutely means &quot;blowing it up.&quot;<br /> <br /> There are times to see the forest, and times to see the trees.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-25T14:16:40-07:00","id":1516128,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TempleBuilder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516111#post1516111\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So every killing (unless it was accidental) would be murder.<br /> ...<br /> It\u2019s justified by practicality,...</div> </div> </div> </div>And here we get to a fundamental problem with classical absolutism. Pretty much the big reason anyone studies Immanuel Kant (also ewwww) is because he astutely observed that absolute moral principles can't have exceptions; not even justifications such as accidents or practicality. Either nuance needs to be built into the absolute principle in the first place, or exceptions are self-refutations of one's own moral principles. As he stated, lying is immoral, and thus it is never justified to lie. If you have taken a victim into your home running from a rampaging murderer, and the murderer comes to your door, Kant argues you cannot morally take the practical way out and lie that the victim is not in your home. If the murderer seeking his next victims asks, you should be truthful, and thus regardless of what transpires next, you acted in accordance with the absolute moral principles.<br /> <br /> There's a reason most people default to a much more utilitarian form of absolutism instead.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I made it, from my understanding of the world.<br /> ...<br /> Morality is not grounded in the real, it\u2019s grounded in the ideal. </div> </div> </div>And another classical moral paradox. Moral principles are born from the real world around us, translated into ideals, and then translated back into moral philosophies.<br /> <br /> The resolution to is issue is that how well a moral principle can be translated back into the real world is inherently a criteria for refining moral concepts. As our moral principles do not simply appear from nothingness in our minds, but rather stem from our lived experiences, they are subject to revision as we experience more. One of the prime reasons philosophers debate so much is that is the crucible of refinement of moral philosophies. The more holes poked in a moral philosophy, the more it must be either refined or rejected as non-viable.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Is it more moral to try for the best outcome? Or more moral to try to avert the worst outcome? </div> </div> </div>As someone that largely falls into the utilitarian umbrella, my answer cannot be made without taking reality on the ground into account. The morality of the two options is dependent on the reality of executing them. I, personally, morally have to try to evacuate people as I lack the skills to defuse a bomb, wasting time trying to defuse a bomb instead of saving as many people as I can would be morally absurd for me to do. A person with the skills to defuse the bomb in time would have a different moral calculus, as would someone with such a bad reputation as a hoaxer that would likely cause more people to stay inside than flee the bomb.<br /> <br /> &quot;Try for the best, &quot; vs. &quot;avert the worst,&quot; are generally meaningless distinctions to me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-25T16:38:36-07:00","id":1516149,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516126#post1516126\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There are times to see the forest, and times to see the trees.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200b<br /> Yeah. For everything there is a season, and this too shall pass. And sometimes the best you can do isn\u2019t good.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516128#post1516128\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And here we get to a fundamental problem with classical absolutism. Pretty much the big reason anyone studies Immanuel Kant (also ewwww) is because he astutely observed that absolute moral principles can't have exceptions; not even justifications such as accidents or practicality. Either nuance needs to be built into the absolute principle in the first place, or exceptions are self-refutations of one's own moral principles. As he stated, lying is immoral, and thus it is never justified to lie. If you have taken a victim into your home running from a rampaging murderer, and the murderer comes to your door, Kant argues you cannot morally take the practical way out and lie that the victim is not in your home. If the murderer seeking his next victims asks, you should be truthful, and thus regardless of what transpires next, you acted in accordance with the absolute moral principles.<br /> <br /> There's a reason most people default to a much more utilitarian form of absolutism instead.</div> </div> </div> </div> Uh, what\u2019s wrong with Kant?<br /> <br /> Anyways, yeah. Absolute moral principles are hard to work with. A lot of people I\u2019ve noticed seem to take a stance of moral mathematicians where the net sum must be positive or at least not negative\u2026maybe moral finances is a better word. I don\u2019t agree with the idea of it \u201cbalancing out\u201d. Each part must be taken on its own terms. As Arcane pointed out though, sometimes the best you can do is lie. But when it comes to killing another, I can\u2019t help but feel like it was a failure, no matter how many were saved in the process.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516128#post1516128\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And another classical moral paradox. Moral principles are born from the real world around us, translated into ideals, and then translated back into moral philosophies.<br /> <br /> The resolution to is issue is that how well a moral principle can be translated back into the real world is inherently a criteria for refining moral concepts. As our moral principles do not simply appear from nothingness in our minds, but rather stem from our lived experiences, they are subject to revision as we experience more. One of the prime reasons philosophers debate so much is that is the crucible of refinement of moral philosophies. The more holes poked in a moral philosophy, the more it must be either refined or rejected as non-viable.</div> </div> </div> </div>Right. And I\u2019d like to take this moment thank you and Arcane for \u201cpeer review\u201d of my patchwork of morals. I do periodically revise mine, since the world is always more nuanced than I knew. I guess I don\u2019t believe anyone really has the answers. Best we as humans can do is hopefully get a closer and closer approximation.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516128#post1516128\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As someone that largely falls into the utilitarian umbrella, my answer cannot be made without taking reality on the ground into account. The morality of the two options is dependent on the reality of executing them. I, personally, morally have to try to evacuate people as I lack the skills to defuse a bomb, wasting time trying to defuse a bomb instead of saving as many people as I can would be morally absurd for me to do. A person with the skills to defuse the bomb in time would have a different moral calculus, as would someone with such a bad reputation as a hoaxer that would likely cause more people to stay inside than flee the bomb.</div> </div> </div> </div> A valid answer.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516128#post1516128\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;Try for the best, &quot; vs. &quot;avert the worst,&quot; are generally meaningless distinctions to me.</div> </div> </div> </div> I\u2019m afraid I don\u2019t see them as meaningless at all. In my experience, they are often are mutually exclusive. You can definitely prevent something bad, or you can maybe do something greater than that. I prefer to try for the best, averting as much as I can without sacrificing the outcome in my opinion which is the best. I guess I would prefer to be foolish and try for a better world than to accept a broken one that could have been better. But is it really moral to do so when people\u2019s lives are on the line?<br /> \u200b\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>To whomever reads this, I hope you have a good day/night. May you be Happy.<br /> <br /> So, I made some Mage Legacies <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1497183-templebuilder-s-new-legacy-thread#post1497183\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>, with some help. They vary in quality, but I hope you take a look at them. Every one contains pieces of me, for better or worse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26736-templebuilder","name":"TempleBuilder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26736_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1251"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-26T12:02:31-07:00","id":1516237,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TempleBuilder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516111#post1516111\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Killing humans is morally unjust in my opinion.</div> </div> </div> </div>If it wouldn't seem leading or bad faith or whatever, I'd like to ask you about some complications with this opinion that jumped right into my mind.<br /> <br /> If you are in a situation in which someone intends to kill you and you lack the ability to incapacitate that person harmlessly, is it moral to let yourself be killed rather than use lethal self-defense, or does that also count as an immoral killing?<br /> <br /> If killing humans is always morally unjust, then doesn't that imply that every has a duty to be constantly practicing unarmed defense techniques in order to avoid ever being put into a kill-or-be-killed scenario? Doesn't it imply that failure to be constantly practicing and improving one's non-lethal self-defense techniques is tantamount to an admission to the intent to use lethal force? And don't people who lack the physical capacity for such self-defense methods form a categorically immoral group of people; like, someone who's wheelchair bound would have to rely on a weapon for self-defense, so aren't people in wheelchairs fundamentally immoral under this &quot;killing humans is always unjust&quot; worldview?<br /> <br /> Actually, forget those questions. I think I'm not really asking these questions specifically. I think those questions are really more examples of the real concern: if killing humans is always unjust, then what judgment should be passed on people who do not <b>or cannot</b> relentlessly pursue the necessary skills and/or equipment to never need to use lethal force in self-defense?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/271-jimb","name":"JimB","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar271_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-26T14:25:33-07:00","id":1516257,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JimB</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516237#post1516237\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If it wouldn't seem leading or bad faith or whatever, I'd like to ask you about some complications with this opinion that jumped right into my mind.</div> </div> </div> </div> Well, I find it important that I can answer these questions, so I don\u2019t mind.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JimB</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516237#post1516237\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you are in a situation in which someone intends to kill you and you lack the ability to incapacitate that person harmlessly, is it moral to let yourself be killed rather than use lethal self-defense, or does that also count as an immoral killing?</div> </div> </div> </div> Yes, I believe it would be more moral to let yourself be killed. I also don\u2019t expect all but the most moral to let that happen to them or their loved ones. Ignoring that most situations will have more options than kill or die, I think most people will fight for what they love even when they arguably shouldn\u2019t. I can\u2019t blame anyone for that, even though I do believe it is the wrong choice from a moral perspective.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JimB</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516237#post1516237\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If killing humans is always morally unjust, then doesn't that imply that every has a duty to be constantly practicing unarmed defense techniques in order to avoid ever being put into a kill-or-be-killed scenario? Doesn't it imply that failure to be constantly practicing and improving one's non-lethal self-defense techniques is tantamount to an admission to the intent to use lethal force?</div> </div> </div> </div> No. It may be unwise, but not immoral. And the ideal world would have no need of such training. I\u2019m not a big fan of if not good, then automatically bad.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JimB</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516237#post1516237\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And don't people who lack the physical capacity for such self-defense methods form a categorically immoral group of people; like, someone who's wheelchair bound would have to rely on a weapon for self-defense, so aren't people in wheelchairs fundamentally immoral under this &quot;killing humans is always unjust&quot; worldview?</div> </div> </div> </div> No, again. While it would be wise of them to practice other skills to allow the defusing of situations, they are under no moral obligation to do so.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JimB</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516237#post1516237\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, forget those questions. I think I'm not really asking these questions specifically. I think those questions are really more examples of the real concern: if killing humans is always unjust, then what judgment should be passed on people who do not <b>or cannot</b> relentlessly pursue the necessary skills and/or equipment to never need to use lethal force in self-defense?</div> </div> </div> </div>\u201dHi, nice to meet a fellow person. How are you doing today?\u201d Or running away from having a conversation. You know, the normal stuff for interacting with people.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>To whomever reads this, I hope you have a good day/night. May you be Happy.<br /> <br /> So, I made some Mage Legacies <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1497183-templebuilder-s-new-legacy-thread#post1497183\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>, with some help. They vary in quality, but I hope you take a look at them. Every one contains pieces of me, for better or worse.","edited":{"userid":26736,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26736-templebuilder","user":"TempleBuilder","date":". 06-26-2023, 06:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26736-templebuilder","name":"TempleBuilder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26736_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1251"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-26T14:45:14-07:00","id":1516259,"title":"","content":"I have to make a note that I find it odd to assert that morality is not grounded in the real, given how it's actually pretty easy to see how a good number of moral ideas are rooted in evolutionary instincts.<br /> <br /> Like, keeping it on the subject of murder, part of the reason why murder is pretty universally regarded as a problem is because human beings, as a animal wired towards socialization because it's evolutionarily advantageous for them to work together, are subsequently wired to have murder of strangers like us be really hard-it's a major hurdle to get over and very consequential to deal with the results of, as a general rule. People in our modern society who have murder of other human beings as part of their expected duties basically have to go through some rough deprogramming/reprogramming in order to be actionable in the event society calls for murder to be done (an ex-friend of mine was once working towards becoming a cop, and at one point in the Hunter game we were having at the time, he described the process like &quot;they're trying to make me a slasher&quot;-a process I assumed was successful to some degree, since then he's become a jail guard, laughed really hard at his own idea of supplying inmates with capsaicin soap, and really couldn't grasp why I was so offended at the idea, and the less I talk about what the military has done to the recognition of humanity in former friends, the happier we will all be). While there's a lot of idealism in ideas of morality and ethics, a lot of it is also just trying to take advantage of what humanity, as animals, was already predisposed to do.<br /> <br /> Morality and ethics are ideas that are, at it's barest, meant to useful short hand for solving problems* in situations where dealing with the complex mess of factors can delay in producing a useful reaction, and it's most is a model we might use to shape our environment into evolving the species into a more &quot;moral&quot; and &quot;ethical&quot; animal, but in addressing that, it has to be acknowledged that nature operates on spectrums** rather than concrete and distinct notions, and as such it's important to remember than any given structure has it's limits of utility, and can ultimately at the end end up as a hinderance. Maybe that wouldn't be a problem if we were ideal creatures, but as creatures of the real, any idea of how we are to evolve ourselves has to have grounding in our reality.<br /> <br /> To assert a divorce of morality and ethics from the real is a bit like spinning a bike tire in the air. Sure, you're getting a lot of motion there, but it's not moving anything.<br /> <br /> *Are you surprised that utilitarian humanism is my primary mode of absurdist confrontation?<br /> **Even this is inaccruate, but keeping it simple here.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-26T15:18:55-07:00","id":1516264,"title":"","content":"I believe TempleBuilder has a post in edit purgatory so I'm not going to add a bunch more right now.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TempleBuilder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516257#post1516257\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, I believe it would be more moral to let yourself be killed.</div> </div> </div> </div>This should probably use some elaboration. Why is purposefully killing an absolute immoral act, but letting people die at the hands of a killer morally good? There's a lot of unpacking to do there from my perspective. What in your life experiences has lead to this belief? What's the core ideal that reconciles the many situations that clearly don't slot neatly into this?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-26T15:48:32-07:00","id":1516269,"title":"","content":"I feel like I also have to briefly mention the utility of contradiction(/&quot;contradiction&quot;) in practical philosophy.<br /> <br /> THis example is gonna fall short, but changing subjects briefly for illustration, I am not a person who particularly believes in free will/free agency, which makes a lot of my commentary seem damnedably weird given how centered on choosing and the operational accountability/consequences of choices it is. An example from Chronicles is that I hold beasts, mages, and particularly hunters as the most monstrous of the splats because of their greater agency to <i>choose</i> and <i>commit </i>to that monstrosity*. How do I maintain a standard of demand for accountability at all if I also correspondingly believe that we're all just protein chains being worked through? THe short answer is accepting that reality in practice can be contradictory, and in this particular case that even if free will doesn't exist, the illusion of free will persists, and if we're all going to be busy lying to ourselves involuntarily like that anyways, we might as well make it play into the pursuit of shaping an environment that will evolve humanity into better forms and dispositions.<br /> <br /> Bringing it back to murder, I think it's actually pretty fair to call murder morally unjust, and more than being fair, it's very valuable-one of the pervasive problems in history is the willingness to rely on murder as a way to solve problems that correspondingly can't, by the nature of it's willingness, provide neccesary counter-activity to handle it. But as I also mentioned, while the murder in of itself, an action that is murder can also yield many great goods that are not invalidated by that one moral injustice (though they absolutely can be, which is why it pays to start from murder as morally unjust), and a refusal to engage with murder in terms of it's utility is pretty well parallel, if not quite entwined, with the &quot;when good [people] do nothing&quot; in the notion of &quot;Evil will triumph when good [people] do nothing.&quot;<br /> <br /> Like, &quot;Murder's basically always wrong&quot; and &quot;If a unjust society that perpetuates great evil rises because you were unwilling to commit neccesary action toward's it's preventation, the evils of that society are, in part, on your hands&quot; can't be mutually exclusive from each other, because strict adherence to either leaves victims in the gutter either way. The contradiction only demands more accountability, it doesn't cause the whole contradiction to self-implode. Two things seemingly in opposition can true if used appropriately in their contexts.** <br /> <br /> *and yes, I am keenly aware of how hilarious that is given the way I approach the horror of being a Beast.<br /> *Though as a Guardian stan in Awakening, I am aware of the slippery slope here as well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-26T16:32:37-07:00","id":1516276,"title":"","content":"Sigh. Why Bot why? It \u2018twas just a single edit to fix a grammatical mistake.<br /> <br /> To answer Heavy Arms question, nothing in my life experiences directly lead to my viewpoints as far as I\u2019m aware. Maybe the persistent set of nightmares I\u2019ve had since I read Ender\u2019s Game could be responsible, I don\u2019t know. (That was my first real exposure to the viewpoints involving murder.) I\u2019ve always been agnostic as long as I can remember, and after many existential crises in my teens over the nature of death, nihilistic. I eventually stabilized on Existentialism a philosophy. Since then I\u2019ve been building a moral philosophy as part of my quest to make my own meaning. And I didn\u2019t think murder was a satisfactory solution to any problem. As such I classify situations where murder is the best solution to be situations without a moral solution.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>To whomever reads this, I hope you have a good day/night. May you be Happy.<br /> <br /> So, I made some Mage Legacies <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1497183-templebuilder-s-new-legacy-thread#post1497183\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>, with some help. They vary in quality, but I hope you take a look at them. Every one contains pieces of me, for better or worse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26736-templebuilder","name":"TempleBuilder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26736_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1251"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-26T18:05:27-07:00","id":1516291,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516259#post1516259\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Like, keeping it on the subject of murder, part of the reason why murder is pretty universally regarded as a problem is because human beings, as a animal wired towards socialization because it's evolutionarily advantageous for them to work together, are subsequently wired to have murder of strangers like us be really hard-it's a major hurdle to get over and very consequential to deal with the results of, as a general rule\u2026While there's a lot of idealism in ideas of morality and ethics, a lot of it is also just trying to take advantage of what humanity, as animals, was already predisposed to do.<br /> \u200b</div> </div> </div> </div> Under this view point would you say my position would be a natural consequence of finding bits of myself in everyone I meet? Because I specifically tried to be empathetic as I can, imagining myself it as other people in my efforts to trying to understand them.\u200b While obviously a useful trait for a social animals, I take that far past where it is reasonable for a natural instinct. I guess that\u2019s the point where becomes ideal. Either way you are correct, my philosophy would not be fully apart from the real.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516269#post1516269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But as I also mentioned, while the murder in of itself, an action that is murder can also yield many great goods that are not invalidated by that one moral injustice (though they absolutely can be, which is why it pays to start from murder as morally unjust), and a refusal to engage with murder in terms of it's utility is pretty well parallel, if not quite entwined, with the &quot;when good [people] do nothing&quot; in the notion of &quot;Evil will triumph when good [people] do nothing.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah. If it becomes a hindrance to helping, is it really moral? I\u2019d like to think that motivating myself to try and find better solutions is beneficial\u2026but in the most terrible of circumstances would I be ineffectual? I believe it is up to everyone to determine what their life means\u2026but is using mine for a moral stand that fails to help others really a moral action? Or is it selfishness? I don\u2019t know.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>To whomever reads this, I hope you have a good day/night. May you be Happy.<br /> <br /> So, I made some Mage Legacies <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1497183-templebuilder-s-new-legacy-thread#post1497183\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>, with some help. They vary in quality, but I hope you take a look at them. Every one contains pieces of me, for better or worse.","edited":{"userid":26736,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26736-templebuilder","user":"TempleBuilder","date":". 06-26-2023, 09:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26736-templebuilder","name":"TempleBuilder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26736_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1251"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-26T18:44:17-07:00","id":1516296,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TempleBuilder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516291#post1516291\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Under this view point would you say my position would be a natural consequence of finding bits of myself in everyone I meet? Because I specifically tried to be empathetic as I can, imagining myself it as other people in my efforts to trying to understand them.\u200b While obviously a useful trait for a social animals, I take that far past where it is reasonable for a natural instinct. I guess that\u2019s the point where becomes ideal. Either you are correct, my philosophy would not be fully apart from the real.<br /> <br /> <br /> Yeah. If it becomes a hindrance to helping, is it really moral? I\u2019d like to think that motivating myself to try and find better solutions is beneficial\u2026but in the most terrible of circumstances would I be ineffectual? I believe it is up to everyone to determine what their life means\u2026but is using mine for a moral stand that fails to help others really a moral action? Or is it selfishness? I don\u2019t know.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is probably a good point to note that philosophy isn't neccessarily about having answers, so much as lens to look at problems for understanding it's many angles, and again this includes moral and ethical philosophy.<br /> <br /> Going with myself here, if you've been tracking some of my argument, you might reach a point of understanding that I believe that having some form of a military is important from a utilitarian point of view, but that every soldier who kills as part of that utility is to be held accountable for murder-which, hey, yeah, true. But how does one justify the creation and maintenance of a organization of what essentially has to be mass murderers if we are then going to say that they are accoubtable for those murders, under the terms of murder being morally unjust? How can you ask someone to do something useful that you will then ask for them to basically then make penance for?<br /> <br /> And that's a good question, and it's one I don't have an answer to if I strictly adhere to both principles. The best I have is proposing something akin to sin-eaters for military roles, but that's still a honking unfair system, even if everyone who joins the military is willing to basically &quot;sin and be judged for that sin, to a just end&quot;<br /> <br /> And to that, I have to concede that position is not enough, even if the foundations I start from can be logically supported. &quot;Murder is a failure of the systems and circumstances that should be mourned when it needs must be the answer&quot; is a useful tool, as is &quot;Injustice must be met with the tools approrpiate to the systems and circumstances at work&quot;, but neither is useful for &quot;A person who fills a role of utility for society deserves respect and support for filling that role as is appropriate&quot;(because the extreme end of that is the hero worship of soldiers we have here in America, and dear god is soldier worship a wildly rampant thought disease I would like to remove).<br /> <br /> I'm not cynical as to say that morality is so relative as to be non-existant, but one of my firm points of view is that morality cannot be fixed if it is to remain moral. When people describe morality as a negative, I usually understand it as moral laws, structures, codes, etc., and I get that, because systems inevitably try to make infinite and eternal ideas and actions rooted in them that is forever going to be mutable in some form or fashion. There are definitely guidelines and priniples that are going to be highly recurrent and reliable, but by and large, there's always going to be a portion of morality that is going to rely on intuition, and with that a willingness to handle justice and mercy on a case-by-case basis, and a willingness to abandon a structural tool when and where it becomes destructive.<br /> <br /> Of course, the counter to that that encourages a setting of limpness and equivocation, where in one can be justified in not being willing to take action or a stance on the grounds that taking action or a stance invariably precludes other answers to a problem. Mindfulness and all that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-26T19:03:42-07:00","id":1516300,"title":"","content":"I suppose I should be plain that I do not think of morality as either a castle that needs protecting even as it protects those who dwell within it, or as an arsenal of tools one uses to build better selves and worlds.<br /> <br /> Morality is a storm.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-26T20:13:30-07:00","id":1516310,"title":"","content":"Something I appreciate in my own journey of building my moral philosophy, is that I find Jewish theology a lot more practical than people think (though I feel like a lot gets lost in translation).<br /> <br /> Judaism, for example, has an interesting solution to the conundrum of soldiers: Jewish morals say that those returning from war must spend a period of separation from normal society before fully returning. This specific transition period is supposed to be spent with other soldiers, and largely overseen by female relatives (that is, mothers, aunts, sisters, etc. not wives). <br /> <br /> On one hand this is a form of accountability for going to war War taints your soul with acts of violence, and you don't get to just come back without atoning for that. On the other hand, this structured transition from military to civilian life is something a lot of modern solider would likely appreciate having in place instead of being left to figure it out themselves. It helps that Judaism is both extremely obsessed with ritual purity, but makes impurity and cleansing a very normal activity so it's not really a punishment to adults; it's the spiritual equivalent of hygiene since there's far too much to avoid it all you just get used to regular cleansing. It follows a model much closer to the modern concept of restorative justice rather than punishment: the whole point is to easy the return from war, not to diminish the sacrifice made for the greater whole.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-26T22:48:45-07:00","id":1516316,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516310#post1516310\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Something I appreciate in my own journey of building my moral philosophy, is that I find Jewish theology a lot more practical than people think (though I feel like a lot gets lost in translation).<br /> <br /> Judaism, for example, has an interesting solution to the conundrum of soldiers: Jewish morals say that those returning from war must spend a period of separation from normal society before fully returning. This specific transition period is supposed to be spent with other soldiers, and largely overseen by female relatives (that is, mothers, aunts, sisters, etc. not wives).<br /> <br /> On one hand this is a form of accountability for going to war War taints your soul with acts of violence, and you don't get to just come back without atoning for that. On the other hand, this structured transition from military to civilian life is something a lot of modern solider would likely appreciate having in place instead of being left to figure it out themselves. It helps that Judaism is both extremely obsessed with ritual purity, but makes impurity and cleansing a very normal activity so it's not really a punishment to adults; it's the spiritual equivalent of hygiene since there's far too much to avoid it all you just get used to regular cleansing. It follows a model much closer to the modern concept of restorative justice rather than punishment: the whole point is to easy the return from war, not to diminish the sacrifice made for the greater whole.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well that's fucking awesome.<br /> <br /> Also evidence that however much research I think I did into Judaism a decade ago, I did not do enough.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-06T08:33:35-07:00","id":1517280,"title":"","content":"So, by the way, because no one pointed it out, what happened with the shift of conversation is a good demonstration of the point that Heavy Arms made a while back-philosophic arguments are better served by starting from the broad moral, ethical, and legal idea (in this case, absolute pacifism and the idea of inherent immorality of all murder) and then working our way towards complicated scenarios that challenge the premise-in this case, wrestling with the contrast against practical and utilitarian implementation of murder against parties who fight against building a moral, just and merciful environment, ran straight into the issue of the unfairness of asking professions that engage with murder to also be accountable for the assumed inherent immorality, and arrived at a cool answer for that. And there's ways that could be built upon even further that would be better served by getting into narrower and narrower examples to test the values of the philosophic set we're building here.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-06T13:17:17-07:00","id":1517306,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TempleBuilder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516149#post1516149\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200bUh, what\u2019s wrong with Kant?</div> </div> </div> </div>The guy that argued becoming a step-mother was such an immoral act that the Golden Rule meant we must be abusive to them as they are naturally abusive to their new husband's preexisting children?<br /> <br /> To cherry-pick a bit less, since one can make a case that Kant wasn't a complete hypocrite, Kant never resolved the dilemma of universalization's ability to apply to mutually contradictory ethical stances. His very weak (or at least what he would have lambasted as such in others and only mildly chided himself over) responses were to shrug his shoulders and say, &quot;well, things are the way they are, which had to have happened for reasons, so we should assume the way things are is the better choice.&quot; Basically, Kant's philosophy can't - on a fundamental level - differentiate between, &quot;it is moral to treat all humans as equal,&quot; and, &quot;it is moral to establish a power hierarchy based on sex/gender, race, and religion.&quot; And his response was to effectively say that if said hierarchies exist and have existed for extended periods, then the better default position is that we can't see why hierarchies that just happen to put people like him at the top are just a natural fact of life than assume that we can make an equitable world (without ever saying an equitable world is impossible, again, he couldn't argue either way directly).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A lot of people I\u2019ve noticed seem to take a stance of moral mathematicians where the net sum must be positive or at least not negative\u2026maybe moral finances is a better word. </div> </div> </div>Utilitarianism is the better word; it's what the man who popularized the concept of &quot;moral calculus&quot; in modern philosophy called philosophy that takes this approach. Though utilitarian thought didn't end with Bentham and Mill. That said, even going back to Mill, utilitarianism doesn't reject your own feelings on these things. Mill calls out the moral calculus itself as a source of harm, because it offers the most moral option possible to us, which exacerbates the impact of seeing the suffering of those we couldn't help. Thus the suffering of taking on the pain of choosing to benefit some at the cost of others needs to be built into a fully thought out utilitarian philosophy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But when it comes to killing another, I can\u2019t help but feel like it was a failure, no matter how many were saved in the process. </div> </div> </div>Ultimately though, are you experiencing an ethical failure, or an emotional failure? We can do lots of things that make us feel like failures that aren't the results of poor moral judgements. I mean, as a self-professed utilitarian, of course to me this just seems like you're making perfect the enemy of good here, but that's cold comfort to the undeniable emotional harm that comes from making choices that result in the deaths of others.<br /> <br /> While heavily influenced by other concepts (I can expound on the Jewish concept of tikkun olam as regards to how I view benefit/harm in a utilitarian context if you'd like), I don't see imperfect results as failures, but they are vital motivations to work towards a world where we can get closer to perfect outcomes. We need to strike a healthy personal balance in our activities of course, but there's a reason why we value looking back at situations that didn't go to expectations (and especially ones that went horribly wrong) to figure out what happened, and what we can do to make sure that doesn't happen again. What soothes the pain of taking a life for the greater good isn't maintaining the current status of the world, but the desire to never experience that pain again leading to productive changes so that such pains can be minimized if not eliminated from the world. So maybe not calling it a failure is some semantic hair splitting, but it's a failure of the world as it exists around us, not a personal failure, to have to make good choices that still have bad consequences.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And I\u2019d like to take this moment thank you and Arcane for \u201cpeer review\u201d of my patchwork of morals. </div> </div> </div>I'm glad you're enjoying this discussion. I always get a bit apprehensive that these things can get a bit fraught.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I\u2019m afraid I don\u2019t see them as meaningless at all. In my experience, they are often are mutually exclusive. You can definitely prevent something bad, or you can maybe do something greater than that. I prefer to try for the best, averting as much as I can without sacrificing the outcome in my opinion which is the best. I guess I would prefer to be foolish and try for a better world than to accept a broken one that could have been better. But is it really moral to do so when people\u2019s lives are on the line?<br /> \u200b\u200b </div> </div> </div>I think I mostly addressed this above, but I feel like the core issue here is the difference between momentary choices, and broad considered goals. I feel like your catastrophizing a bit by focusing on the extremes of lives being on the line. If you're in a moment of imminent choice - regardless of the stakes - you can only act on the information you have at hand. You can't see the Nth order consequences that will spread from your actions. Worrying about things beyond the scope of the moment are morally paralyzing. Regardless of how events ripple away from an action, we can't live our lives effectively is we see each moment of choice as what makes or breaks the whole world. Taking one life to save ten lives doesn't not permanently concede to a world where taking lives to save lives in necessary. That's why I reject the distinction. Averting the worse now can let me try for the best later, and vice versa (if more rarely). I don't see the usefulness of a mode of thought where averting the worse in the moment is inherently anathema to ever trying for the best.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1516316#post1516316\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well that's fucking awesome.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, it's a good start anyway. It's also not something specific to Judaism, as lots of ancient cultures practice some form of ritual cleansing for returning soldiers. That said, there's a lot of cultural stuff layered on top of these principles that needs to be addressed or recontextualized before applying it to a wider group of people or modern social constructs.<br /> <br /> It's also a fairly obscure thing based on a short passage in Numbers, that hasn't been applicable in over 2,000 years as strictly constructed.<br /> <br /> What I feel is the big thing to look at, which exists as a wider through-line in Jewish social theology, is that there's a need for communities to create healthy transitional periods for members undergoing times of stress. By making ritual out of what's a completely arbitrary period of time to move from one place in life to another, it at least provides a base-line... and normalcy and stability for people to latch onto because there is no objectively right answer. How long to mourn, how long to atone, how long to hold on to grudges (or pursue forgiveness)... Judaism has all these customs and rituals where the numbers given aren't really the point, but rather the idea of having an answer at all is. because sometimes that's what people need, or at least sometimes that's what they need to start the process of identifying what more they need.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also evidence that however much research I think I did into Judaism a decade ago, I did not do enough. </div> </div> </div>To paraphrase one Rabbi, if someone ever says they've researched enough, toss their writings in the trash. And to paraphrase another, how much is enough? One whole-assed cow.<br /> <br /> I realize fully contextualizing that is a bit too much of explaining the joke, but there's a lot of self-referential and self-deprecating humor in Jewish theological texts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-06T20:25:24-07:00","id":1517328,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1517306#post1517306\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The guy that argued becoming a step-mother was such an immoral act that the Golden Rule meant we must be abusive to them as they are naturally abusive to their new husband's preexisting children?</div> </div> </div> </div> Ah. I guess I never heard that particular story. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1517306#post1517306\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ultimately though, are you experiencing an ethical failure, or an emotional failure? We can do lots of things that make us feel like failures that aren't the results of poor moral judgements. I mean, as a self-professed utilitarian, of course to me this just seems like you're making perfect the enemy of good here, but that's cold comfort to the undeniable emotional harm that comes from making choices that result in the deaths of others.</div> </div> </div> </div> I see it as both. One could argue that I\u2019m somewhat biased, given that I\u2019m basing a moral in part on a deep emotional response, but I don\u2019t think my emotional response takes away from the ethical concerns. <br /> <br /> I believe in redemption and atonement as being something anyone can do. People change, it\u2019s just how the world works. Many, when confronted with their own wrongdoing, will clamp down on whatever they felt justified their actions. Some will never truly believe they were in the wrong, even onto their end. But rare as it may be, some won\u2019t. Some will try to make an amends. Some will go on to do great good. For example, I\u2019ve read of a conquistador who one day realized the scope of the horrors he had inflicted. He became a priest, tried to end slavery wherever he could. He even went back to Spain, and though he didn\u2019t manage change public opinion on Spanish Colonialism or the humanity of those they had conquered, he is considered a founder of Spanish civil rights. If even the worst of us can try to make amends, couldn\u2019t anyone? Killing someone takes away all they could be, all the good they could have done. It inflicts grief and trauma on the loved ones of the killed, and in many way harms those who kill. And of course, very few want to die. Knowing all of that, I just can\u2019t say it\u2019s moral to kill. It may be the most practical solution, maybe even the \u201cbest\u201d solution, but it\u2019s just not one I can accept as moral.<br /> <br /> From an emotional standpoint, I think it\u2019s well established that I have a strong aversion to even the idea of killing somebody. But there is also a more self serving reason. I also am prone to obsession, fixation on topics. It\u2019s nice when it\u2019s a video game I like, or something like the life cycles of jellyfish, or talking about morals. It\u2019s less nice when being unable to stop trying to imagine what death and dying is like, or the think about implications of the heat death of the universe. In my experience\u2026it hasn\u2019t mixed well with guilt or grief. I try to make amends, even if I\u2019m the only one who feels like I was at fault. Sometimes I just make things worse. Given how strongly I react just to the thought experiments, and how hard it is to break out of my obsessions, I\u2026don\u2019t know if I would survive the experience. Even if I did, I feel like something key to who I am would break.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1517306#post1517306\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While heavily influenced by other concepts (I can expound on the Jewish concept of tikkun olam as regards to how I view benefit/harm in a utilitarian context if you'd like)</div> </div> </div> </div> Please do. I am very curious.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1517306#post1517306\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't see imperfect results as failures, but they are vital motivations to work towards a world where we can get closer to perfect outcomes. We need to strike a healthy personal balance in our activities of course, but there's a reason why we value looking back at situations that didn't go to expectations (and especially ones that went horribly wrong) to figure out what happened, and what we can do to make sure that doesn't happen again. What soothes the pain of taking a life for the greater good isn't maintaining the current status of the world, but the desire to never experience that pain again leading to productive changes so that such pains can be minimized if not eliminated from the world. So maybe not calling it a failure is some semantic hair splitting, but it's a failure of the world as it exists around us, not a personal failure, to have to make good choices that still have bad consequences.</div> </div> </div> </div> I think our differences in opinion on this part is mostly semantics. Is it a failure of the world? Or a failure of people to meet the world\u2019s challenges? You favor the former, I favor the latter. Both, I think, are valid interpretations of the imperfections of the world. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1517306#post1517306\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm glad you're enjoying this discussion. I always get a bit apprehensive that these things can get a bit fraught.</div> </div> </div> </div> Yeah. I see that a lot, especially online. But the way I see it, everyone is lost in the dark, trying to make sense of the world. People might or might not have pieces of the truth, but nobody has the full picture, especially me. So I try to listen, try to see if I can learn something from others. You can find wisdom anywhere. Even the worst people, even they aren\u2019t aware of it, have wisdom, something that one can learn from. Granted they probably won\u2019t like the take away, but non the less, they have it. So I try not to judge, and I try to be nice. I think that\u2019s helped smooth a lot of situations that could have gone a lot worse.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1517306#post1517306\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, it's a good start anyway. It's also not something specific to Judaism, as lots of ancient cultures practice some form of ritual cleansing for returning soldiers. That said, there's a lot of cultural stuff layered on top of these principles that needs to be addressed or recontextualized before applying it to a wider group of people or modern social constructs.<br /> <br /> It's also a fairly obscure thing based on a short passage in Numbers, that hasn't been applicable in over 2,000 years as strictly constructed.<br /> <br /> What I feel is the big thing to look at, which exists as a wider through-line in Jewish social theology, is that there's a need for communities to create healthy transitional periods for members undergoing times of stress. By making ritual out of what's a completely arbitrary period of time to move from one place in life to another, it at least provides a base-line... and normalcy and stability for people to latch onto because there is no objectively right answer. How long to mourn, how long to atone, how long to hold on to grudges (or pursue forgiveness)... Judaism has all these customs and rituals where the numbers given aren't really the point, but rather the idea of having an answer at all is. because sometimes that's what people need, or at least sometimes that's what they need to start the process of identifying what more they need.</div> </div> </div> </div>I need to look more into this. It\u2019s weird how much sense that makes. I wonder what overlap a more modern version would have with therapy. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1517306#post1517306\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To paraphrase one Rabbi, if someone ever says they've researched enough, toss their writings in the trash. And to paraphrase another, how much is enough? One whole-assed cow.</div> </div> </div> </div> I really like those quotes. Yes, absolutely. There is so much nuance to the world. One could study anything for their entire life, and they would have learned as much as one who drifts from topic to topic. And nobody has the full picture.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>To whomever reads this, I hope you have a good day/night. May you be Happy.<br /> <br /> So, I made some Mage Legacies <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1497183-templebuilder-s-new-legacy-thread#post1497183\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>, with some help. They vary in quality, but I hope you take a look at them. Every one contains pieces of me, for better or worse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26736-templebuilder","name":"TempleBuilder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26736_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1251"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-07T02:34:05-07:00","id":1517340,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TempleBuilder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1517328#post1517328\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I see it as both. One could argue that I\u2019m somewhat biased, given that I\u2019m basing a moral in part on a deep emotional response, but I don\u2019t think my emotional response takes away from the ethical concerns.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, to reemphasize what I was trying to say, I don't think emotional responses shouldn't be part of ethical concerns. Just that they shouldn't be the overriding factor of ethical concerns.<br /> <br /> While the Internet has thoroughly ruined the Trolley Problem, the Trolley Problem assumes the actor is some sort of coldly rationale Vulcan-wannabe. One of the failings of such thought experiments is that it doesn't take into account what the consequences for the person capable of changing the course of the trolley car would be. Part of the transition from classical to modern utilitarianism is ceasing to treat the actor in such thought exercises as immune to the harm, pain, and suffering they endure during and after the event. Adding that variable to the moral math makes what are more cut and dry utilitarian responses in classical formulations much less so.<br /> <br /> My reasoning for not really abiding by the idea of emotions being too potent in ethical evaluations though, is that they run into the same problem as Kant's universalization principles. Going just on emotions runs afoul of the broad range of human emotions. For myself, I know I don't have the moral repulsion to killing you do. While I have fortunately not had to kill anyone, I have been in a situation where I had to make the choice of use lethal force in self-defense, but was saved by others rushing to the scene before anything irreversible happened. But having been in that moment. Having made the choice (and I was quite conscious of it, it wasn't some heat of the moment lose of control) to put my own life over another person's life... I felt nothing. I didn't feel good. I didn't feel bad. I don't have an emotional attachment to other human beings to be a pacifist of any stripe without feeling intellectually dishonest. I have to come at this from an intellectual perspective because my emotional self would snuff out a life as easily as snuffing a candle in pushed into the right circumstances without any negative emotional responses.<br /> <br /> I've tried. I can't make myself feel guilt for that. Trying to feel bad about something humans are supposed to feel bad about didn't do anything but reduce my self-image of myself as a human and as someone deserving considerations as a human; a beast among the flock as it were. Of course I can change, but I have to change around that immutable part of myself. The idea of living in a community of absolute pacifists scares me, because there would be no check on me if I was placed in a position where violence felt like an emotionally acceptable response. I need a moral structure where emotions count, but aren't the only thing that guides my behavior.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Killing someone takes away all they could be, all the good they could have done. It inflicts grief and trauma on the loved ones of the killed, and in many way harms those who kill. And of course, very few want to die. Knowing all of that, I just can\u2019t say it\u2019s moral to kill. It may be the most practical solution, maybe even the \u201cbest\u201d solution, but it\u2019s just not one I can accept as moral. </div> </div> </div>This does go back to letting yourself die, or letting other people die because you refuse to take a life to save lives as a moral option. Letting someone (even yourself) die when you don't have to squanders all of the human potential that using lethal force to stop the killer too. If the core moral objection is the permanent lose of potential by ending a life, the line between killing on purpose and killing by inaction aren't really important because the loss of potential is the same in either case.<br /> <br /> If something can be the best solution but still immoral, it feels like an impossible moral standard to live by.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Please do. I am very curious. </div> </div> </div>OK, so the phrase &quot;tikkun olam&quot; roughly translates into &quot;fixing the world.&quot; It's a phrase that has morphed significantly in scope and cultural meaning over time, but the fundamental core is that the world is flawed, and we have a duty to work to make it better. To quote a more modern phrasing by Edmond Fleg (a poet and playwright that was actually raised in a traditional Jewish family in Switzerland, but moved to France and lived a much more secular life before reestablishing a connection to Judaism later in life) from &quot;Why am I a Jew?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;...<br /> I am a Jew because the promise of Israel is the universal promise.<br /> I am a Jew because, for Israel, the world is not completed; we are completing it.<br /> I am a Jew because, for Israel, humanity is not created; we are creating it.<br /> I am a Jew because Israel places humanity and its unity above the nations and above Israel itself.\u200b<br /> ...&quot;<br /> <br /> Though to note this was written in 1927, and &quot;Israel&quot; refers to the Jewish people rather than the modern country of Israel.<br /> <br /> The full poem is a bit longer, and rather poignant, but this section gets to the heart of what matters to my own perspectives. In classical utilitarian thought there's a lot of debate around how to value pleasure/good and pain/harm. Bentham was rather binary about things that very much clashed with most people's lived experiences, while Mill tried to incorporate more subjective merits to things. Bentham would say to destroy a priceless cultural artifact if it meant providing the best materials to shelter humans in need, while Mills argued that the balance of good and harm can accept slightly less optimal living conditions to preserve unique things that can cause good events for ages to come.<br /> <br /> The modern Jewish perspective cuts through a lot of that debate by having this preexisting concept of &quot;fixing&quot; the world. Being in a constant state of improving the world, and not just for our personal selves, or our personal communities, but for everyone, there's a much deeper concept of what to weigh in moral judgements when balancing immediate needs vs. future goals. Fleg's last quoted line is the most vital: the unity of humanity overrides the concept of individual nations and peoples within it. Working to a world where all people are one collective unit is more important than many classical utilitarians could really express, because utilitarianism doesn't really come with that sort of built in end goal to be striving for. It doesn't answer what to life for, just how to live until you find it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I wonder what overlap a more modern version would have with therapy. </div> </div> </div>There's a lot of what could be perceived as precursors to cognitive behavioral therapy and interpersonal therapy in Jewish social theology. A lot of this comes from Judaism not really caring much about the afterlife (which I think a lot of people find odd considering how much Christianity and Islam do care about it). In Jewish theology, the default state of humanity is to get into the &quot;good&quot; version of the afterlife Only extremely specific and egregious actions can get you banned from that beyond any redemption. As such, there's a strong focus on doing what you can with the life you have. We all end up in the Messianic Era together the same. So a lot of these ritualized socialization are very focusing on the here and now: resolving trauma, developing coping mechanisms, providing tools for less personally disruptive reactions to mental health issues, etc. Judaism doesn't propose people are innately damaged and need fixing or dire results await, just that life breaks people enough you need to make sure they can make it through with hope and dignity so we can arrive together at what comes next.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I really like those quotes. Yes, absolutely. There is so much nuance to the world. One could study anything for their entire life, and they would have learned as much as one who drifts from topic to topic. And nobody has the full picture. </div> </div> </div>Well, the cow bit is more of a very old, &quot;go touch grass,&quot; bit of snark. There's more to life that study and debate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-09T22:24:23-07:00","id":1517626,"title":"","content":"<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1517340#post1517340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For myself, I know I don't have the moral repulsion to killing you do. While I have fortunately not had to kill anyone, I have been in a situation where I had to make the choice of use lethal force in self-defense, but was saved by others rushing to the scene before anything irreversible happened. But having been in that moment. Having made the choice (and I was quite conscious of it, it wasn't some heat of the moment lose of control) to put my own life over another person's life... I felt nothing. I didn't feel good. I didn't feel bad. I don't have an emotional attachment to other human beings to be a pacifist of any stripe without feeling intellectually dishonest. I have to come at this from an intellectual perspective because my emotional self would snuff out a life as easily as snuffing a candle in pushed into the right circumstances without any negative emotional responses.</div> </div> </div> </div> I'm sorry you had to go through that. I'm also glad everyone made it out ok. Emotions aren't always what we want them to be. What's important is to be honest and true to yourself, and it sounds like you got that in hand.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1517340#post1517340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've tried. I can't make myself feel guilt for that. Trying to feel bad about something humans are supposed to feel bad about didn't do anything but reduce my self-image of myself as a human and as someone deserving considerations as a human; a beast among the flock as it were. Of course I can change, but I have to change around that immutable part of myself. The idea of living in a community of absolute pacifists scares me, because there would be no check on me if I was placed in a position where violence felt like an emotionally acceptable response. I need a moral structure where emotions count, but aren't the only thing that guides my behavior.</div> </div> </div> </div> Oh Heavy, I'm sure you know this, but you are very much human. Everyone is kinda broken. And that's ok. Humans in general are just really bad at being human. Don't let anyone tell you that you aren't a person, not even yourself. And I don't think that is something that needs to change, nor would should anyone demand it of you. As pointed out, an unwillingness to kill can be a blessing as a motivation to find other solutions, but it can also be a hindrance in situations where it is necessary. As Arcane has said, it's important not to deny the good, along with the bad. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1517340#post1517340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This does go back to letting yourself die, or letting other people die because you refuse to take a life to save lives as a moral option. Letting someone (even yourself) die when you don't have to squanders all of the human potential that using lethal force to stop the killer too. If the core moral objection is the permanent lose of potential by ending a life, the line between killing on purpose and killing by inaction aren't really important because the loss of potential is the same in either case.</div> </div> </div> </div> Yes, that's true. Letting others die because you chose not to kill isn't very moral, in the end. But I believe that people can choose where their potential goes. Through our choices on what we think matters, we give our life its meaning. And that includes choosing to die. Maybe that sounds strange to you if you want, I can explain more.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1517340#post1517340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If something can be the best solution but still immoral, it feels like an impossible moral standard to live by.</div> </div> </div> </div> I think you could say that's part of the point. I don't see failure as necessarily the end of the world. Something to strive to prevent, but as Arc said/quoted earlier, sometimes you can be perfect and still lose. That's life. Failure and imperfection is unavoidable, what's important is being able to learn, and do better. But perfection is something worth striving for, even when it's impossible to truly achieve.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1517340#post1517340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The full poem is a bit longer, and rather poignant, but this section gets to the heart of what matters to my own perspectives....The modern Jewish perspective cuts through a lot of that debate by having this preexisting concept of &quot;fixing&quot; the world. Being in a constant state of improving the world, and not just for our personal selves, or our personal communities, but for everyone, there's a much deeper concept of what to weigh in moral judgements when balancing immediate needs vs. future goals. Fleg's last quoted line is the most vital: the unity of humanity overrides the concept of individual nations and peoples within it. Working to a world where all people are one collective unit is more important than many classical utilitarians could really express, because utilitarianism doesn't really come with that sort of built in end goal to be striving for. It doesn't answer what to life for, just how to live until you find it.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's a beautiful excerpt. Clearly, I need to look at Judaism next. I do think, one day, we'll do it. We will make a good, kind world for us all. It's going to be a long time, and our natures will certainly have changed, but we can do it. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1517340#post1517340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, the cow bit is more of a very old, &quot;go touch grass,&quot; bit of snark. There's more to life that study and debate.</div> </div> </div> </div> What, study isn't everything? Clearly, I need to feel more grass...Astro Turf will do, right? That way I won't even need to leave the house!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>To whomever reads this, I hope you have a good day/night. May you be Happy.<br /> <br /> So, I made some Mage Legacies <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1497183-templebuilder-s-new-legacy-thread#post1497183\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>, with some help. They vary in quality, but I hope you take a look at them. Every one contains pieces of me, for better or worse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26736-templebuilder","name":"TempleBuilder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26736_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1251"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-10T00:21:10-07:00","id":1517629,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TempleBuilder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1513630-questions-reflections-about-law-and-moral-philosophy?p=1517626#post1517626\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Humans in general are just really bad at being human.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I would put it that humans are generally just really bad at living up to our preconceptions of what humanity is. The instincts I learned the rough way how to tap into are part of being human. We all have those things buried under layers and layers of other instincts, socialization, indoctrination, etc. The fact that I have a relatively rare knowledge of how to cut through all of that for myself should I feel the need arises doesn't make me bad at being human - those things are part of being human; even if it doesn't really help me live up to the potential humans can be striving for. Though it's not without it's benefits in the long run. I've found myself having a much keener idea of why some rules work while other rules fail to curb negative behavior. I've learned how to tap into that momentary state of not caring about rules out of obligation or duty to society, and think about reasons why a rule might still work, or wouldn't work at all, to someone else not in a state of mind to care about a rule being the rule on the grounds of it simply existing that way. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Don't let anyone tell you that you aren't a person, not even yourself. </div> </div> </div>Oh, if only it was that simple. But that's getting a bit beyond this thread, or what I'm comfortable getting into regarding my own mental health journey in public. That said? Cogito ergo sum. I am. Even if what I am could somehow objectively be measured and found that I fall out of the bounds of &quot;human&quot; or &quot;person&quot; I still am something of its own value as much as those words are.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Maybe that sounds strange to you if you want, I can explain more. </div> </div> </div>It doesn't sound strange to me. I'm very much of the opinion that modern humanism has but too much stress on people breathing as long as possible, not necessarily living as long as possible. But I'm not very convinced a life-and-death situation where very little full consideration towards one's potential can be made, is own where choosing death can really be made earnestly.<br /> <br /> Though, to keep up with my own theme here, another theological tidbit that I tend to hold to: &quot;We are commanded to extend life, but it is a sin to prolong death.&quot; If someone full believes taking an action is tantamount to the death of their humanity, forcing them to torture themselves doesn't mesh with my own moral constructs.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Failure and imperfection is unavoidable, what's important is being able to learn, and do better. </div> </div> </div>But when does failure that we learn from as a motivator to do better next time turn into immorality? Why does it turn into immorality if that could risk halting this learning process? It seems to me the more consistent moral principle would not be that killing is always immoral, but that failing to use that to reduce future suffering is when it becomes fully so.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Astro Turf will do, right? </div> </div> </div>Can it feed that cow that needs to eat?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}